{
 "id": 196,
 "created_at": "2026-10-29T19:19:11.413962+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dd1JPnNB6AI/",
 "title": "Why sending free samples is not real marketing",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator contrasts two business approaches: one owner who blindly courier-mailed free samples to fifty food pages and only received three basic thank-you stories, and another owner who built a tight-knit community of ten people with a WhatsApp group. The core lesson is that sending random free samples is just a courier service, whereas making people feel like they are part of your team builds true brand advocates.",
 "key_points": [
  "A murukku brand owner sent free sample boxes to fifty food pages on Instagram and only got back three 'Thanks for sending!' stories.",
  "The other forty-seven influencers ate the product, liked it, and moved on to the next parcel.",
  "Another business owner in the same town selected only ten people and created a WhatsApp group for them.",
  "New items go to the ten selected people first for tasting, and items change monthly based on their feedback.",
  "The photo of the top seller is framed on the shop wall.",
  "The ten community members actively defend the brand in comments, acting like an internal team rather than clients.",
  "The creator claims that sending samples and praying is not marketing; it is a courier service.",
  "Making people feel like they are on your team creates true marketing, which costs far more than a free product.",
  "Samples buy a post, but community builds a creator."
 ],
 "content": "50 samples, 3 stories. He sent free samples to fifty influencers. He got back three thank-you stories and one big hole in his pocket. A murukku brand owner. Any food page he saw on Instagram, he'd courier them a box. He sent to fifty pages. Result? Three people posted a story: \"Thanks for sending!\" Done. The other forty-seven? They ate it, it was nice, and the next day they moved on to the next parcel. In the same town there's another aunty. She picked just ten people. She has a WhatsApp group for them. Any new item goes to them first for tasting. One call a month and one item changes based on their feedback. The photo of the top seller is framed on the shop wall. Now those ten people are fighting in the comments to defend the brand, man. To them it's not a client. It's their own team. Sending samples and praying isn't marketing, it's a courier service. The feeling of \"you're on our team\" is marketing. That costs far more than a free product, which is why nobody gives it. Samples buy a post. Community buys a creator. Thank you.",
